/// A fixed-arity overload beats a variadic one.
///
/// Variadic is the fourth rung below exact, widening and Any, so Pick('x')
/// selects the String overload and only Pick with two arguments reaches the
/// variadic.  Other languages describe this as a separate resolution phase
/// tried after the first fails; written as one more rung it needs no phases and
/// the ladder stays single.
///
/// ⚠️ Without that ordering these two declarations would be ambiguous on every
/// one-argument call, and a language cannot ask the programmer to disambiguate
/// what it offered to infer.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ')' after parameters.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
function Pick (S : String) : String;
begin
    Exit 'one';
end

function Pick (S : String...) : String;
begin
    Exit 'many:' + Str (S.Length);
end

test 'A fixed-arity overload beats a variadic one';
begin
    AssertEqual (Str ('one'),     Pick (Str ('a')));
    AssertEqual (Str ('many:2'),  Pick (Str ('a'), Str ('b')));
    AssertEqual (Str ('many:0'),  Pick ());
end
